-Two approaches remain credible:
+Two revisions with identical visit fields are still two revisions, and a worker's own stale-based edit competes like anyone else's. The record store makes no exception that would require it to judge whether two submissions mean the same thing.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Submission
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+A submission carries:
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+- visit ID (client-generated UUID)
+- idempotency key
+- base revision ID — the revision the worker's edit started from; absent only when the worker created the visit
+- the complete visit fields: plot ID, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The author is taken from the authenticated worker, not from the payload. Any authenticated worker may submit a revision of any visit.
+
+Before accepting, the record store validates field types, that the plot ID resolves in the site catalog (archived plots resolve), and that a supplied base revision ID names a revision of the same visit. A submission that fails validation is rejected with a reason and stores nothing; the tablet still holds the draft.
+
+An accepted submission returns the new revision ID, the visit's resulting status, and, when the status is `in_review`, the IDs of the revisions in the competing set. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns that original result, even if the visit's status has since changed; reuse of a key with a different payload is rejected.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+**Current state** of a visit returns its status. For a `settled` visit it returns the current revision. For a visit `in_review` it returns the competing revisions and no current revision; callers must not treat any one of them as the visit's state.
+
+**Revision history** of a visit returns every revision in receipt order, each with its base revision ID, author, and receipt time, and every resolution record. Any value that reaches an export can be traced through this history to the submissions and coordinator decisions behind it.
+
+**Listing by status** returns the visits that are `settled` or the visits that are `in_review`.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves a visit in review by naming the competing revision IDs they reviewed and giving one outcome:
+
+- **Select** one of the competing revisions as it stands, or
+- **Replace** them with a complete set of visit fields the coordinator supplies, which is stored as a new revision authored by the coordinator.
+
+The outcome revision becomes current and the visit becomes `settled`. The record store keeps a resolution record: coordinator, time, the competing revision IDs, the outcome revision ID, and an optional note. Revisions that were not selected stay in the history.
+
+The named revision IDs must equal the visit's competing set at the moment of resolution. If a further revision arrived while the coordinator was reviewing, the resolution is rejected and the coordinator reviews the enlarged set. The same check rejects a repeated resolution, because the visit is no longer in review. Only coordinators may resolve.
+
+A tablet that still holds a draft based on a revision that was not selected submits it like any other edit: its base is not the current revision, so the visit returns to review with the resolved revision and the new one competing.
+
+## Obligations on Dependent Nodes
+
+- **tablet-sync** stores the base revision ID with each local draft, sends complete visit fields rather than changes, and shows the worker when a submission was accepted into review. It needs no base snapshots.
+- **review-export** presents each visit in review as a comparison of its competing revisions, submits resolutions naming the revisions shown, and exports only `settled` visits.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions; competing revisions go to a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge: combine disjoint field changes automatically and review only overlaps | A merge can combine scientifically related edits (a species identification and its cover estimate) without noticing, and the server cannot infer those relationships. Merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries would need ongoing maintenance by a team without a systems administrator. The cost accepted is a larger review queue: edits to unrelated fields of the same visit also need a coordinator. |
+| State of a visit in review | No usable state; competing revisions returned side by side | Keep serving the previously current revision; serve the most recent revision | Either alternative lets one worker's revision stand as the visit's state on arrival order alone, and would let an unreviewed value reach an export. |
+| Membership of the competing set | Latest revision of each line of edits; a revision based on a competing revision replaces it | Every revision received during review competes | A worker who fixes their own pending revision should not enlarge the coordinator's comparison. The replaced revision stays in the history. |
+| Resolution outcomes | Select a competing revision, or replace with coordinator-supplied fields | Select only | Competing revisions often each hold a valid correction to different fields. Without a replace outcome the coordinator must discard one and re-enter it through a tablet. The combination is a human judgment recorded with its author, not a server rule. |
+| Resolution against a changed competing set | Reject; coordinator re-reviews | Apply to the revisions named and leave the newcomer competing with the outcome; apply and drop the newcomer | Dropping discards a submitted edit unseen. Applying partially is correct but produces a second review of the same visit immediately; rejecting keeps one decision per review. |
+| Identical or same-author competing revisions | Compete like any others | Collapse identical revisions; let an author's later revision supersede their earlier one | Both alternatives are comparison rules the server would own. A coordinator dismisses an identical pair in seconds. |
